**Deviation flagged first:** `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` sections 3.1/3.2 were *not* actually rewritten in this worktree (no staged or unstaged diff on that file) despite the task box saying so. I implemented from the task's own detailed summary (self-consistent and precise enough to serve as spec), then wrote the doc rewrite myself afterward so code and docs agree, and updated the B2 completion box's requirements list too.

**Occurrence-memo representation:** keyed by `[source-eid stack]`, `stack` the literal innermost-first vector of enclosing parameter vectors (plain Clojure vectors of symbols compare and hash correctly by value, so no derived representation was needed). `resolve` now builds the resolved tuples as a fresh tree walk (not a `mapcat` over the input datoms): every occurrence mints a fresh negative id (deterministic counter, first-visit order) via `visit!`/`emit-node!` in `debruijn_resolve.cljc`, memoized so an equal-context repeat reuses the id and does not re-walk; a repeat under an unequal context mints a distinct id and independently resolves. Every structural child ref in the emitted tuples is rewritten to the child's resolved id, never the source id.

**Cycle detection:** an `active` set of source entities currently on the DFS path, checked *before* the memo lookup in `visit!`, matching `yin.vm.debruijn/project-node`'s exact behavior and diagnostic shape: `(ex-info "Cyclic AST reference" {:rule :cycle, :entity e, :path path})`. Test: `cyclic-input-is-refused` in `debruijn_resolve_test.cljc`, a hand-built two-entity cycle (`lambda` body → `application` operator → same `lambda`).

**Inverse's merge-by-source step:** `unresolve` walks the resolved tree via `unresolve-node!`, translating each record back to its source id through `:source`; a `seen` set (keyed by source entity) makes emission idempotent — the first record of a shared source entity emits its facts, every later record of the same source just returns the id without re-emitting or re-recursing. Tested by `unresolve-inverts-resolve-over-the-corpus` (now over 3 args: `tuples source params`) plus two new fixtures added to the corpus map... actually the shared-entity fixtures are separate defs (`shared-variable-under-two-contexts`, `shared-lambda-under-two-contexts`) exercised by their own tests (`a-shared-variable-resolves-per-its-own-context`, `a-shared-lambda-resolves-per-its-own-context`) and confirmed via direct REPL checks that `(= original-datoms (unresolve (:tuples r) (:source r) (:params r)))` for both.

**validate-resolved's checks:** tree shape (via `vm/index-datoms`'s `error`/`root-id`), every structural child ref resolving to a known record (`:dangling-ref`), no record carrying both a bound and a free resolution (`:ambiguous-resolution`), the existing tree-level scope check unchanged (`:scope`, reusing `walk-scope`/`variable-scope-defect`), and `source` total over the `:yin/type`-bearing entities `tuples` names (`:source-total`). `resolve` calls it on its own output before returning; `lower-stack` calls it unconditionally at entry before doing anything else.

**Host-value-rejection scope:** extended the checks (not the docstring), per item 4's finding. Added `reject-host-value!` calls for `:yin/name`, `:yin/params`, `:yin/op`, `:yin/buffer`, `:yin/prefix`, `:yin/parked-id` — every data operand `yin.vm.linearize/lower`'s `emit!` would refuse, closing the P2 gap glm-5.3 found.

